<mujoco model="bittle">
  <!-- ============================================================
       Cleaned, unified Bittle MJCF for RL use (Genesis or MuJoCo)
       Based on:
         - bittle_scaled.xml  (geometry / dynamics, correctly scaled)
         - bittle_task.xml    (actuators, sensors, keyframe)
       Removed:
         - <include "../common.xml">  (external dependency)
         - MJPC <custom> blocks       (planner-specific)
         - Mocap goal body            (MJPC-specific)
         - Terrain props (ramp, hill, box)  (not needed for RL)
         - <user> sensors             (MJPC cost terms only)
       Fixed:
         - Servo torque limit:  0.55 -> 0.18 N*m  (matches real Petoi P1S/P2S)
         - Position controller: added kp=20, kv=0.5  (was implicit kp=1)
         - Actuator ctrlrange:  matches actual joint ranges per joint
         - Mesh path:           meshdir="assets" + plain filenames (was double-prefixed)
         - Added a ground plane (so the robot has somewhere to land)
         - Added an accelerometer sensor for full IMU
         - Collision set: the four foot spheres are the only colliding
           geoms; every mesh geom is rendering-only. See the <default> block below
           for what this replaced and why. This removes the mesh-vs-mesh narrowphase
           that used to make MJX take 30+ minutes to JIT-compile one rollout, so the
           "replace each collision mesh with a box in a derived XML" workaround an
           earlier revision used is no longer needed - there is nothing left to box.
         - Foot sphere position: at the actual toe, the far end of
           each shin along the knee link's local x. Previously the spheres never made
           contact at all and the knee mesh took every one. See the footL/footR block
           for the axis mistake this went through first.
         - Left/right symmetry: the right legs are exact mirrors of
           the left. They were not before, and had not been since bittle_scaled.xml:
           the right toes stood 11mm wider and 2mm shorter at identical joint angles,
           because neither the placement transforms nor the meshes were mirrored
           (right knee body carried a z=+0.003996 the left lacked; left_knee.002 has
           690 verts against right_knee.002's 686, with a 2.3mm bounding-box
           difference along the shin). That is CAD-export residue, not design:
           github.com/ger01d/opencat-gym models/bittle_esp32.urdf, an independent model of the same
           robot, is exactly mirrored on round numbers (shoulders at +-0.05, knees at
           +-0.015/-0.045). An asymmetric model teaches an asymmetric gait.

           Each right body/geom/inertial transform is now the left one reflected
           through x=0 as (p, R) -> (M p, M R M) with M = diag(-1,1,1), and the right
           meshes are the left OBJs mirrored in their own frame (*.mirror.obj, faces
           rewound so normals stay outward). Joint axes stay "0 0 -1": the reflection
           reverses rotation sense, so a negated axis keeps +0.56 rad meaning the same
           stand pose on both sides. Verified: L/R toe mismatch is 0 um across 400
           mirrored joint poses (was 11000 um), inertias mirror exactly, and the
           settled stance loads all four feet instead of three.
       ============================================================ -->

  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="assets" autolimits="true"/>

  <option timestep="0.005" cone="elliptic" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <default>
    <!-- frictionloss must stay small relative to the 0.18 N*m actuator forcerange below: it is
         Coulomb friction the motor has to overcome before the joint moves at all, so a value at
         or above the torque limit locks the joint outright. Was 0.1 (56% of stall torque, and
         domain randomization's U(0.5,2.0) scaling pushed it to 0.2 > 0.18, locking joints in
         ~68% of randomized robots). 0.03 is ~17% of stall torque, in line with typical micro
         servo gearbox stiction, and stays at 8-33% of torque across the full randomized range. -->
    <joint limited="true" damping="0.01" armature="0.007" frictionloss="0.03"/>
    <!-- Position-actuator defaults: stiffer than original (kp=1 was too soft),
         force-limited to realistic micro-servo torque (0.18 N*m ~ 1.8 kg*cm).

         kv 0.35 and joint armature 0.007: the actuator here must
         be a fast-but-not-weightless tracker, because the servo's response is modelled
         SEPARATELY by mj_vec_env._apply_actuator_dynamics, which shapes ctrl before it
         reaches this actuator. The pair is what the policy experiences, so the pair is what
         gets fitted -- against the STEP measurement, which is the thing being reproduced:
         measurements/reference/step_summary.csv joint 8 reads dead time 22.0 ms + tau 84.1 ms
         (t63 ~106 ms) and 165.9 deg/s on the large step. Composed here: t63 120 ms, no
         overshoot, slew 151 deg/s.

         Two failed settings are worth recording, because they bracket this one.
         kv=0.5/armature=0.01 (the original) composes to t63 140 ms -- ~30% slow against the
         step measurement. kv=0.1/armature=0.0002 was an attempt to hit t63 40 ms, a target
         taken from the bodyid tau of 33 ms; but bodyid is a frequency response measured with
         the servo continuously driven and its own docstring warns against using it for
         absolute gains, so that fitted a step response to the wrong one of two disagreeing
         methods (SIM2REAL_NOTES: "where two methods disagreed, we covered both"). It also
         made the joints ~6x lighter than the physical links, and they rang on every footfall:
         with one fixed policy, the >5 Hz share of true dof_vel while walking went 2% (0.01)
         -> 78% (0.0002) -> 4% (0.007), with forward speed +0.100 -> +0.056 -> +0.097 m/s.
         A suspended-leg step fit cannot see that, which is why the walking spectrum is now
         part of how this pair is chosen.

         kp and forcerange are untouched: kp sets tracking stiffness under load and forcerange
         is the believed stall torque, both load-path properties the filter does not model.
    -->
    <position kp="20" kv="0.35" gear="1" forcelimited="true" forcerange="-0.18 0.18"/>
    <equality solref="0.01 1.1"/>
    <!-- Nothing collides by default. Every mesh geom in this file is rendering-only: each link
         carries TWO coincident copies of its mesh (a named "*_c" one and an unnamed rgba one),
         and the only intended contact surface is the four foot spheres, which opt back in via
         class="footL"/"footR" below.

         BEWARE the pairing here. Written as contype=1/conaffinity=2, every "*_c"
         geom additionally overrode its own class="visualgeom" with an inline
         contype=1/conaffinity=15 - so BOTH mesh copies on every link collided, the exact
         opposite of what the surrounding comments claimed. Compiled, that produced 164 candidate
         collision pairs, 108 of them mesh-vs-mesh self-collision (including 4 pairs between two
         21,714-vertex convex hulls), and 8 floor contacts at the "home" keyframe where there
         should be 4 - each link touching the floor twice through its own duplicate, doubling
         effective contact stiffness. That is what made a single MJX training rollout take 30+
         minutes to JIT-compile, and what forced the Genesis env's max_collision_pairs=100.
         With this default the model compiles to 4 candidate pairs (foot sphere vs floor).

         Consequence to be aware of: the body and legs can now pass through the floor if the
         robot tips far enough. The env terminates at 10 deg of roll/pitch, long before any part
         other than a foot could reach the ground, so this never arises during training. -->
    <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
    <default class="visualgeom">
      <geom material="visualgeom" contype="0" conaffinity="0" friction="0.9 0.02 0.01"/>
    </default>
    <!-- The four foot spheres are the ONLY colliding geoms in this file (contype=1/conaffinity=2
         against the floor's contype=2/conaffinity=1; the Genesis env's separately-added
         gs.morphs.Plane uses the same pair).

         pos is set PER GEOM below, not here: the four toes are at genuinely different points
         (the sides are mirrored OBJs, and front/back differ by ~0.2mm).

         How the toe is located, and the trap to avoid: the knee link's long axis is its local
         **x**, not z. In the knee-link frame the mesh spans x [-0.0548, +0.0108] (65.6mm, the
         shin), y 16mm, z 27.4mm, and world-"up" at the stand pose is [0.900, 0.436, 0]. So the
         toe is the far end of the shin at x = -0.0548 (left) / +0.0544 (right) - NOT the mesh's
         minimum z. Each sphere centre is that toe vertex offset by one radius along world-up,
         which puts its surface exactly on the tip.

         History, all three attempts wrong on the same axis:
           - original z=-0.0825: left over from an unscaled coordinate system.
           - rescaling it 0.1x to z=-0.00825 lands inside the mesh's z-range, but the
             spheres still never touched the ground, so this class's priority/condim=6/friction
             tuning was dead code and mjx_env.py's foot-height contact test could never fire.
           - z=-0.0208, derived from the mesh's minimum z *in the link
             frame*, which is meaningless because the link is rotated at the stand pose. That put
             the contact point ~4cm UP THE SHIN, so the knee meshes hung 4.1cm below the floor and
             the robot visibly walked on its knees. It also dragged the stand height down to
             0.0533. Corrected here.

         Note this means the pre-fix model was contacting the ground at roughly the right place:
         the knee mesh's tip IS the toe. What was actually wrong was that the *sphere* took no
         part in it, that both mesh copies collided (doubling every contact), and that the mesh
         collision brought 108 mesh-vs-mesh self-collision pairs with it. Those are what this
         file's collision rework fixes; the stand height (~0.094) barely moves.

         `size` (5mm radius) is deliberately NOT rescaled; it is already a plausible real toe-pad
         size at this scale.

         solimp is MuJoCo's default 0.9 0.95 0.001, not the exporter's 0.015 1 0.031: another
         constant borrowed from a much larger quadruped, in the same family as the frictionloss
         and push-magnitude bugs noted elsewhere in this repo. d0=0.015 means near-zero constraint
         impedance at first touch, ramping to full only over 31mm of penetration - fine for a
         30kg machine, absurd for a 0.257kg robot pressing 0.63N per foot. It went unnoticed
         because the knee mesh (MuJoCo-default, stiff solimp) took every contact while the foot
         spheres never touched anything at all; the moment the feet engaged, the robot sank >12mm
         into the floor. With the default it settles ~0.3mm in, at z=0.09382 against a geometric
         0.09436.

         friction[0], measured at 0.25 rather than the exporter's 0.80: the last constant with nothing
         behind it, and it was 3-5x too high. Inclined-plane test, the robot reading its own
         incline off its accelerometer (bittle_io/experiments/friction.py, traces in
         measurements/reference):

             smooth plastic   slip at  8.8 deg  ->  mu_s 0.156   (5 trials, 0.142-0.182)
             fabric mat       slip at 23.8 deg  ->  mu_s 0.440   (2 clean trials, 0.435/0.445)

         The feet are BARE PLASTIC - Bittle X ships no rubber pads - so hard plastic on a smooth
         floor really does let go near 9 degrees. The old 0.80 randomized to 0.48-1.12, i.e. the
         entire trained range sat ABOVE the grippiest surface this robot has ever stood on, so
         foot slip was a phenomenon no policy had ever encountered. Nominal 0.25 sits between the
         two surfaces and allows for kinetic < static; mj_vec_env randomizes the feet over
         0.10-0.55 ABSOLUTELY rather than scaling this.

         What this does NOT explain, tested rather than assumed: sweeping mu over 0.80/0.44/0.25/
         0.156 under trained policies (64 robots, 6 s, randomization off) moves forward
         speed by only ~6% (0.108 -> 0.100 m/s) and leaves gait
         cadence bit-identical at 1.17 / 1.33 Hz. So the hardware reports of a faster tempo and
         of "moving in place" are NOT caused by this, and the plausible-sounding story - stance
         foot slides out backwards instead of driving the body - is simply not what the physics
         does at these speeds. The gait-tempo gap belongs to the dof_pos observation mismatch
         (sim observes true joint angles, hardware observes a servo-model estimate: 1.17 -> 4.65
         Hz and +0.079 -> -0.020 m/s). This correction is worth making because the number was
         wrong, not because it fixes the symptom.

         Only the foot value matters at contact: priority=1 makes MuJoCo take the foot geom's
         friction outright instead of combining it with the floor's. -->
    <default class="footL">
      <geom type="sphere" size="0.005"
            contype="1" conaffinity="2"
            priority="1" solimp="0.9 0.95 0.001" condim="6" friction="0.25 0.02 0.01"/>
    </default>
    <default class="footR">
      <geom type="sphere" size="0.005"
            contype="1" conaffinity="2"
            priority="1" solimp="0.9 0.95 0.001" condim="6" friction="0.25 0.02 0.01"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <!-- Skybox + ground texture (purely cosmetic, safe to drop if Genesis complains) -->
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0"
             width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge"
             rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3" markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8"
             width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true"
              texrepeat="20 20" reflectance="0.2"/>

    <!-- Robot meshes (scale 0.1 = the "scaled" file's correction factor) -->
    <mesh name="base_frame.001"   file="base_frame.001.obj"   scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="battery.001"      file="battery.001.obj"      scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="cover.001"        file="cover.001.obj"        scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <!-- Head assembly from MarcHesse/bittle-mujoco (MIT), meshes originally from
         PetoiCamp/ros_opencat. Authored in MILLIMETRES -- their model applies
         <mesh scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/> via a <default> block, which is easy to miss when
         reading only the <mesh> declarations. Without it MuJoCo reads them as metres and the
         head renders 1000x oversized, dragging the scene bounds so far the robot vanishes
         from frame. Ours has no such default, so the scale is stated explicitly per mesh. -->
    <mesh name="head__1"       file="head__1.obj" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="jaw_1"         file="jaw_1.obj" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="c_neck__1"     file="c_neck__1.obj" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="servo_neck__1" file="servo_neck__1.obj" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="left_knee.002"    file="left_knee.002.obj"    scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="shoulder.004"     file="shoulder.004.obj"     scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="left_knee.003"    file="left_knee.003.obj"    scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="shoulder.007"     file="shoulder.007.obj"     scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="right_knee.002.mirror" file="right_knee.002.mirror.obj" scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="shoulder.005.mirror" file="shoulder.005.mirror.obj" scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="right_knee.003.mirror" file="right_knee.003.mirror.obj" scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <mesh name="shoulder.006.mirror" file="shoulder.006.mirror.obj" scale="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>

    <material name="visualgeom" rgba="1.0 1.0 0.0 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Lighting + ground -->
    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" specular="0.2 0.2 0.2"
           directional="true" castshadow="false"/>
    <!-- Finite (not "0 0" infinite) so the groundplane checker texture's texrepeat below tiles at
         a scale actually visible relative to this ~10cm robot; an infinite plane renders as a
         1000m quad in Genesis, which at texrepeat="5 5" makes each checker square ~200m wide
         (invisible at this scale). 2x2m with texrepeat 20x20 gives 10cm squares. -->
    <geom name="floor" pos="0 0 0" size="2 2 0.05" type="plane"
          material="groundplane" condim="3" friction="0.9 0.02 0.01" contype="2" conaffinity="1"/>

    <!-- Robot body hierarchy: copied verbatim from bittle_scaled.xml -->
    <body name="root" pos="0 0 0.092" quat="1 0 0 0">
      <site name="imu" size="0.001" pos="0 0 0"/>
      <!-- Head assembly: PURELY COSMETIC. Replaces the primitive boxes that stood in for
           it; these are the real Petoi meshes (MarcHesse/bittle-mujoco, MIT, originally
           PetoiCamp/ros_opencat), so the pointy-eared silhouette is the actual geometry.

           Frame transform: their model is X-FORWARD, ours is Y-FORWARD, so quat is +90 deg
           about z (x->y, y->-x). In their file every head geom's pos exactly cancels its
           accumulated body position, i.e. all four meshes are authored in one shared
           base_link frame -- which is why a single shared transform places all of them.
           The z offset aligns their base_link origin with our root, derived by matching the
           SHOULDER JOINT ANCHORS in both models rather than assuming the origins coincide.

           Physics impact: none, by construction.
             * mass="0" -- the real head's mass is already inside the 193.5 g root inertial
               (which includes their 18.7 g neck servo), so adding mass would double-count.
             * class="visualgeom" -- non-colliding, and _build_model strips it before training.
           Note the real robot's neck joint is not modelled: we do not drive the head servo,
           so the head is rigidly fixed to the torso here.

           HEAD ALIGNMENT: yaw 90 -> 115.25 deg, pos.x 0 -> +0.01960.

           As shipped the head was yawed ~24 deg to the right about the neck. The large pos.x
           is not a second defect -- MuJoCo rotates a geom about its OWN origin, not about the
           neck, so correcting the yaw swings the whole head sideways and the offset brings it
           back. Fixing rotation and position separately does not converge; they have to be
           solved together.

           Solved by searching yaw and pos.x directly, rebuilding the model at each candidate
           so MUJOCO performs every transform, and scoring the two points that must both sit
           on the centreline:

               neck base x    snout x
               original      +0.00 mm    +22.45 mm
               solved        +0.10 mm     -0.03 mm

           Verified against cameras DEFINED IN THE MODEL on the x=0 plane, so the image centre
           is that plane by construction rather than by an assumed azimuth convention.

           Five earlier attempts failed, every one by trusting a measurement without checking
           what it measured: an mjvCamera azimuth whose image "up" was never checked against
           +y; a search range that excluded the answer; a nearest-neighbour "symmetry" score
           that is near zero for ANY dense cloud overlapping its mirror; an analysis over raw
           mesh_vert, which is pre-transform and not what MuJoCo places; and hand-composed
           rotation matrices that silently produced non-yaw quaternions. Letting the simulator
           do the transform and scoring two named landmarks is what finally worked.

           Cosmetic only: all four geoms are mass="0" class="visualgeom" (contype=0/
           conaffinity=0) and _build_model deletes them before training, so no policy has ever
           seen them and nothing here can affect a run. -->
      <geom name="head_c" type="mesh" mesh="head__1" pos="0.01500 0.01000 -0.02500"
            quat="0.456064 0.138759 0.072233 0.876090" mass="0" class="visualgeom"/>
      <geom name="jaw_c" type="mesh" mesh="jaw_1" pos="0.01500 0.01000 -0.02500"
            quat="0.456064 0.138759 0.072233 0.876090" mass="0" class="visualgeom"/>
      <geom name="neck_c" type="mesh" mesh="c_neck__1" pos="0.01500 0.01000 -0.02500"
            quat="0.456064 0.138759 0.072233 0.876090" mass="0" class="visualgeom"/>
      <geom name="neck_servo_c" type="mesh" mesh="servo_neck__1" pos="0.01500 0.01000 -0.02500"
            quat="0.456064 0.138759 0.072233 0.876090" mass="0" class="visualgeom" rgba="0.15 0.15 0.15 1"/>
      <freejoint/>
      <!-- Torso mass, CoM and inertia. Composed from MarcHesse/bittle-mujoco (MIT), whose
           meshes and component masses come from PetoiCamp/ros_opencat and were validated
           against a physical unit (273.5 g total).

           HISTORY, because two of these were wrong in different directions:
             1. Originally mass=0.165, diaginertia="0.001 0.001 0.001" -- three identical
                round numbers, i.e. a placeholder, and a total model mass of 257 g, below
                Petoi's own 265-290 g spec.
             2. Then "corrected" to a uniform-box estimate, 1.804e-4 / 7.175e-5 / 2.248e-4.
                That was 3-5x TOO LOW and worse than the placeholder it replaced. A uniform
                box is a poor model here: the mass sits at the extremes -- four shoulder
                servos at the corners, the neck servo 66 mm forward, the battery low -- and
                that distal mass dominates the tensor.
             3. Now composed properly: sum the torso-fixed bodies (frame 77 g + battery 55 g
                + neck servo 18.7 g + 4 shoulder servos 10.7 g = 193.5 g) via the parallel
                axis theorem about their common CoM, then rotate their X-forward frame into
                our Y-forward one (+90 deg about z, so Ixx and Iyy exchange).
           CoM origins were aligned through the shoulder joint anchors, which exist in both
           models, rather than by assuming the body origins coincide. -->
      <inertial pos="-0.0009 0.0065 -0.0076" mass="0.1935"
                diaginertia="8.0017e-04 3.5846e-04 6.9298e-04"/>
      <geom name="base_frame_c" type="mesh" mesh="base_frame.001" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
      <geom type="mesh" rgba="1.0 1.0 0.0 1" mesh="base_frame.001"/>
      <geom name="battery_c" pos="0.000047 -0.000917 -0.004848" quat="1 0 0 0" type="mesh" mesh="battery.001" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
      <geom type="mesh" rgba="0.0 0.0 0.0 1" mesh="battery.001" pos="0.000047 -0.000917 -0.004848" quat="1 0 0 0"/>
      <geom name="cover_c" pos="0.00014 0 0.01251" quat="1 0 0 0" type="mesh" mesh="cover.001" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
      <geom type="mesh" rgba="0.0 0.0 0.0 1" mesh="cover.001" pos="0.00014 0 0.01251" quat="1 0 0 0"/>

      <body name="left-back-shoulder-link" pos="-0.044596 -0.051923 -0.002102" quat="0.499998 0.5 -0.500002 -0.5">
        <inertial pos="0 -0.024575 0.004372" mass="0.006" diaginertia="2.034e-06 1.708e-07 2.091e-06"/>
        <joint name="left-back-shoulder-joint" pos="0 0 0" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.5708 1.6" actuatorfrcrange="-1000 1000"/>
        <geom name="left_back_shoulder_c" pos="0 -0.024575 0.004372" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002" type="mesh" mesh="shoulder.004" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
        <geom type="mesh" rgba="1.0 1.0 0.0 1" mesh="shoulder.004" pos="0 -0.024575 0.004372" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002"/>
        <body name="left-back-knee-link" pos="0 -0.049172 0">
          <inertial pos="-0.008998 0 -0.014638" mass="0.014" diaginertia="2.310e-06 6.403e-06 4.520e-06"/>
          <joint name="left-back-knee-joint" pos="0 0 0" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.22173 1.48353" actuatorfrcrange="-1000 1000"/>
          <geom name="left_back_knee_c" pos="-0.008998 0 -0.014638" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002" type="mesh" mesh="left_knee.002" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
          <geom type="mesh" rgba="0.0 0.0 0.0 1" mesh="left_knee.002" pos="-0.008998 0 -0.014638" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002"/>
          <geom name="left_back_foot" class="footL" pos="-0.05024 -0.00037 -0.01349"/>
          <site name="left_back_foot" pos="-0.05024 -0.00037 -0.01349" type="sphere" size="0.005"/>
        </body>
      </body>

      <body name="left-front-shoulder-link" pos="-0.044596 0.052264 -0.002102" quat="0.499998 0.5 -0.500002 -0.5">
        <inertial pos="0 -0.024575 0.004372" mass="0.006" diaginertia="2.034e-06 1.708e-07 2.091e-06"/>
        <joint name="left-front-shoulder-joint" pos="0 0 0" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.5708 1.6" actuatorfrcrange="-1000 1000"/>
        <geom name="left_front_shoulder_c" pos="0 -0.024575 0.004372" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002" type="mesh" mesh="shoulder.007" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
        <geom type="mesh" rgba="1.0 1.0 0.0 1" mesh="shoulder.007" pos="0 -0.024575 0.004372" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002"/>
        <body name="left-front-knee-link" pos="6e-06 -0.049172 0">
          <inertial pos="-0.009232 0 -0.014614" mass="0.014" diaginertia="2.311e-06 6.400e-06 4.516e-06"/>
          <joint name="left-front-knee-joint" pos="0 0 0" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.22173 1.48353" actuatorfrcrange="-1000 1000"/>
          <geom name="left_front_knee_c" pos="-0.009232 0 -0.014614" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002" type="mesh" mesh="left_knee.003" group="1" class="visualgeom"/>
          <geom type="mesh" rgba="0.0 0.0 0.0 1" mesh="left_knee.003" pos="-0.009232 0 -0.014614" quat="0.499998 -0.5 0.5 0.500002"/>
          <geom name="left_front_foot" class="footL" pos="-0.05048 -0.00037 -0.01347"/>
